AWS Learning Roadmap
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Step 1: Foundation Services
These are the absolute basics — think of them as the CPU, hard drive, and operating system of the cloud.
Amazon EC2 → Virtual servers for running applications.
Amazon S3 → Object storage for files, backups, and media.
Amazon RDS → Managed relational databases (like MySQL, PostgreSQL).
IAM (Identity & Access Management) → Securely manage users and permissions.

Step 2: Monitoring & Scaling
Once you know the basics, move to services that help you manage and scale.
CloudWatch → Monitoring and logging.
Auto Scaling → Automatically adjust EC2 capacity.
Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) → Distribute traffic across servers.

Step 3: Advanced Storage & Databases
Amazon DynamoDB → NoSQL database.
Amazon Aurora → High-performance relational database.
Amazon Glacier → Archival storage for long-term backups.

Step 4: Application & Serverless
AWS Lambda → Run code without servers (serverless).
Amazon API Gateway → Manage APIs for apps.
Amazon ECS/EKS → Container orchestration (Docker/Kubernetes).

Step 5: Security & Compliance
AWS KMS → Encryption key management.
AWS Shield & WAF → Protect against DDoS and web attacks.
AWS Config → Track compliance and resource changes.

Step 6: Analytics & AI
Amazon Redshift → Data warehousing.
Amazon Athena → Query data directly from S3.
Amazon SageMaker → Build and train ML models.
Amazon Recognition → Image/video analysis. 

🧭 Suggested Learning Path
Start with EC2, S3, RDS, IAM, VPC → Core infrastructure.
Add CloudWatch, Auto Scaling, ELB → Monitoring & scaling.
Explore Lambda & API Gateway → Modern serverless apps.
Dive into DynamoDB & Aurora → Advanced databases.
Finish with Analytics & AI → Specialized services.

👉 This roadmap ensures you build confidence layer by layer. By the time you reach Step 6, you’ll be comfortable with both infrastructure and innovation services.
